WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.620
Secure CI/CD continues after artifacts are built.

2
00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:11.880
Deployment automation should use environment gates, release approvals, staged rollout where appropriate, deployment windows, production separation, and audit trails.

3
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:17.020
Sensitive environments deserve stronger controls than development or test systems.

4
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.080
A failed check should stop the release path clearly instead of being silently skipped.

5
00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:26.500
The pipeline should make a blocked release obvious and explainable.

6
00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:29.260
Rollback planning should happen before release.

7
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:40.080
Teams should know which artifact can be restored, which configuration or feature flags may need to change, which database or migration considerations apply, and who can approve a rollback.

8
00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.420
The point is not to expect failure.

9
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.960
The point is to keep recovery from being invented under pressure.

10
00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:50.140
Rollback steps should be tested or reviewed enough that they are credible.

11
00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:53.220
Monitoring makes the delivery system observable.

12
00:00:53.320 --> 00:01:09.080
Useful signals include build status, test results, artifact identity, dependency warnings, SBOM generation status, signing events, deployment events, release versions, approval records, failed promotions, and unusual job behavior.

13
00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:14.780
These signals help teams distinguish normal release friction from something that needs investigation.

14
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.480
They also help leaders see whether release controls are working as intended.

15
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.720
Incident response depends on evidence.

16
00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:36.380
A good pipeline can answer which source revision built the release, which dependency set was used, which job produced the artifact, which job signed it, who approved promotion, where it was deployed, and how it can be disabled or rolled back.

17
00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.760
Preserving that evidence turns CI/CD from a black box into a reliable release history.

18
00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:49.620
It also helps teams communicate clearly with support, operations, and leadership during high-pressure events and follow-up reviews.

19
00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:52.120
Practice makes those answers faster and calmer.
